<%# How Sherlock reads trackers, in one place: the onboarding welcome, the landing page and the privacy
    page. user: nil on public pages. %>
<%# locals: (user: nil) %>
<% sites = user ? Site.available_to(user).to_a : Site.everyone.where.not(tier: :unsupported).to_a %>
<% if sites.any?(&:instance_key?) %>
  <p><strong class="font-medium text-ink">Don't share your tracker API keys with anyone.</strong> Sherlock doesn't need them: it reads your
    tracker profile with its own key, once you opt in by adding a Sherlock link to it. It sees only what other members of the tracker
    see, so your paranoia settings apply. Sherlock shows your stats only to you.</p>
  <p>For trackers that don't allow this, run your own Sherlock with your own API key. It's simple
    <%= link_to "on your own Debian server", "#{Rails.configuration.x.source_url}#install-on-debian-without-docker", class: "link", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>.</p>
<% else %>
  <p>You add one API key per tracker. Sherlock only reads your stats with it.</p>
  <p>Your keys are encrypted. The person running Sherlock could decrypt them in theory, but the code doesn't, except to send each key to its own tracker.</p>
<% end %>
